1
00:00:03,440 --> 00:00:04,320
Welcome to Launched.

2
00:00:04,480 --> 00:00:10,400
I'm Charlie Chapman, and today I'm excited to bring you the developer and CEO of Astropad, Matt Rongi.

3
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:24,640
Matt, welcome to the show.

4
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:26,000
Yeah, thanks for having me on.

5
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:28,880
I am very excited about this Astropad.

6
00:00:29,200 --> 00:01:01,120
Like, I don't know, I feel like I've said this a couple of times, but there's certain things that to me are like these things that when I entered this world were like these institutions and Astropad for me is this like as someone who came from the at least amateur graphics world and I have a little somewhere right here actually I have a little Wacom tablet and that for me was like the promise of this Apple ecosystem with the iPad and being able to plug all these things in.

7
00:01:01,360 --> 00:01:05,600
Astropad was one of those pieces that I just thought was one of the coolest things ever.

8
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:14,080
And so I'm very excited to talk about this because it's a whole different world I feel like that you live in with all this hardware than a lot of the people I've talked to.

9
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:15,600
Yeah, yeah, no, definitely.

10
00:01:15,680 --> 00:01:17,200
And thanks for the kind words.

11
00:01:17,520 --> 00:01:17,600
Yeah.

12
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:23,840
So before we get into Astropad itself, I'd like to give everyone your background that kind of led you up to Astropad.

13
00:01:24,160 --> 00:01:27,200
So the three questions I ask everybody is, where are you from?

14
00:01:27,760 --> 00:01:29,920
Do you have a formal education related to what you do?

15
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:34,800
And then just kind of a quick tour of what your career was leading up to Astropad.

16
00:01:35,360 --> 00:01:35,760
Yeah, sure.

17
00:01:36,160 --> 00:01:39,840
So, well, I'm originally from the Chicago area.

18
00:01:40,960 --> 00:01:41,840
That's where I grew up.

19
00:01:42,240 --> 00:01:48,720
And I also went to school at the University of Illinois, Urbana Champagne, where I studied computer science.

20
00:01:49,280 --> 00:01:49,360
there.

21
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:55,280
So yeah, I do have a background in computers, computer science.

22
00:01:55,920 --> 00:01:57,200
Fellow, fellow Midwestern.

23
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,800
Yeah, fellow, but are you in Saint Louis?

24
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:02,240
I'm in Saint Louis, yeah, yeah.

25
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:03,440
Yeah, great, yeah, yeah.

26
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:06,640
So not too far actually from Urbana Champagne then.

27
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:07,200
Yeah, no.

28
00:02:07,520 --> 00:02:08,320
I went to school there.

29
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:11,520
Now I actually live in the Minneapolis area.

30
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:14,880
So still in the Midwest, but no longer in the Chicago area.

31
00:02:15,520 --> 00:02:16,400
A lot more snow now.

32
00:02:17,040 --> 00:02:20,960
A lot more snow, colder, but a lot of, a lot of pretty lakes though.

33
00:02:21,920 --> 00:02:23,040
Yeah, that is definitely true.

34
00:02:24,720 --> 00:02:28,640
So what led me to get in here with AstraPad?

35
00:02:28,960 --> 00:02:31,840
Well, I really cut my teeth.

36
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:34,880
I'm a long time Mac developer, long time Mac developer.

37
00:02:35,840 --> 00:02:51,840
Really got involved in the shareware scene in the late nineties, early 2000s, and developed some shareware utilities, sold some of those online on my own website, had some modest amount of success doing that.

38
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:54,000
Just little, little utilities.

39
00:02:54,160 --> 00:02:55,920
One of them was Ghost Hunter and Castoff.

40
00:02:56,160 --> 00:03:00,640
They were like Ghost Hunter was like a file explorer that showed invisible files.

41
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:03,280
This was on the classic macOS.

42
00:03:03,520 --> 00:03:03,920
Oh, wow.

43
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:04,400
Okay.

44
00:03:05,120 --> 00:03:06,480
So this was going back a long way.

45
00:03:07,120 --> 00:03:09,680
So I've been a long time dabbler on the Mac.

46
00:03:10,080 --> 00:03:14,000
Also dabbled a lot with web design, dabbled a lot with graphic design, Photoshop.

47
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:32,720
That was really my intro to computers before I got into programming, it was really kind of more on the graphic design side of things, which is also why Astrapad was always very attractive to me as a product to work on because of that background I had and that fun I had had exploring around on the kind of art design side of things.

48
00:03:33,280 --> 00:03:39,680
Well, and that definitely fits with, I feel like, the, at least, the culture and ethos in early Mac days, especially.

49
00:03:40,240 --> 00:03:41,600
Absolutely, absolutely.

50
00:03:42,560 --> 00:03:45,600
And so did that for some time.

51
00:03:46,960 --> 00:03:57,440
You know, kept working on programming and in college, like I mentioned, I went to University of Illinois and in college I ended up doing two internships at Apple.

52
00:03:58,080 --> 00:03:58,560
Oh, nice.

53
00:03:59,680 --> 00:04:06,800
I worked on the QuickTime team on the QuickTime video pipeline and also on the iPhoto team.

54
00:04:07,040 --> 00:04:08,880
I guess now would be the photos app today.

55
00:04:09,360 --> 00:04:09,360
Wow.

56
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:10,000
Yeah.

57
00:04:10,400 --> 00:04:12,000
So like to interject really quick?

58
00:04:12,320 --> 00:04:12,880
Sure, no.

59
00:04:13,280 --> 00:04:16,320
QuickTime, probably, what time period are we talking about?

60
00:04:16,560 --> 00:04:18,480
This was 2006, 2007.

61
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:22,080
Okay, so like, I guess it was right before that.

62
00:04:22,880 --> 00:04:25,680
That was my entry into the Mac as a platform.

63
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:26,400
Oh, cool.

64
00:04:26,720 --> 00:04:33,200
A friend's dad got a Mac, and I found out that you could make videos with QuickTime directly, you know?

65
00:04:33,440 --> 00:04:36,800
I mean, you still can now, but it was definitely easier before the big switch over.

66
00:04:37,520 --> 00:04:42,240
And so that team, I guess, is a big part of why I'm where I'm at today.

67
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:45,520
So I guess, thank you and your colleagues.

68
00:04:46,640 --> 00:04:48,960
Yeah, I had a very small, small team.

69
00:04:48,960 --> 00:04:54,240
It was a small piece, a part to play, but yeah, there was a bunch of teams, a lot of people working on QuickTime.

70
00:04:54,400 --> 00:04:58,000
You know, the audio team, the video team, the codec team, the math team.

71
00:04:58,240 --> 00:04:58,320
Yeah.

72
00:04:58,640 --> 00:04:59,040
There was a lot.

73
00:04:59,280 --> 00:05:06,640
I don't know what it looks like today, because that was quite a long time ago now, but there was a lot of us working on it, and I learned a ton.

74
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:15,120
I learned a ton about audio and video and how video codecs work and all those, those sorts of things, which would later become really helpful for Astropath.

75
00:05:15,280 --> 00:05:15,520
I bet.

76
00:05:16,320 --> 00:05:18,000
Which we can get to in a bit.

77
00:05:18,800 --> 00:05:31,840
But so I did, I did that internship there and actually was in corporate housing where they like to set you up with an apartment as an intern because it can be really costly even back then to find something in the Bay Area.

78
00:05:32,720 --> 00:05:42,960
And I got paired up with Giovanni Danelli in corporate housing there who came from Italy to do an internship also at Apple.

79
00:05:44,240 --> 00:05:45,520
And that's how we met.

80
00:05:45,680 --> 00:05:48,880
And actually today, he's the cofounder with me of Astrapad.

81
00:05:49,520 --> 00:05:51,600
So we met many years ago at Apple.

82
00:05:52,000 --> 00:06:00,800
And he had also had a similar background too with dabbling with the shareware scene on the Mac, been a long time Mac developer, interested in the graphic design side of things on the Mac.

83
00:06:03,280 --> 00:06:04,880
And that's how we originally met.

84
00:06:05,200 --> 00:06:07,760
But after that, he went full time at Apple.

85
00:06:08,880 --> 00:06:11,520
I decided that Apple wasn't the right place for me.

86
00:06:11,840 --> 00:06:12,720
I had learned a ton.

87
00:06:13,600 --> 00:06:16,320
It would have, it was always like a huge dream to be able to work there.

88
00:06:17,360 --> 00:06:20,800
But, you know, I really knew my heart was in starting my own thing.

89
00:06:21,680 --> 00:06:24,080
I had gotten a taste of it with the shareware business.

90
00:06:25,520 --> 00:06:31,840
And I knew that if I went to a place like Apple, it was, well, I would learn a ton.

91
00:06:32,160 --> 00:06:34,960
It was also going to teach me how to work within a big company.

92
00:06:35,200 --> 00:06:35,280
Yeah.

93
00:06:35,600 --> 00:06:38,560
Which was going to be completely differentely different than starting your own thing.

94
00:06:39,920 --> 00:06:49,360
Because the amount of polish and the amount of Apple's approach to products while they make great products, you can't take that approach wholesale and try to do it as a really small company.

95
00:06:49,600 --> 00:06:51,760
Like Apple just has so many more resources.

96
00:06:52,320 --> 00:06:53,360
It's a completely different ball.

97
00:06:53,920 --> 00:06:55,520
It's a very different skill set, I feel like.

98
00:06:56,160 --> 00:06:57,360
Very different skill set, yeah.

99
00:06:58,560 --> 00:07:04,640
And so I ended up going to a startup that spun out of the University of Illinois, worked there for a while.

100
00:07:05,440 --> 00:07:07,280
I went there instead because it was really small.

101
00:07:07,520 --> 00:07:09,040
It was under ten people getting started.

102
00:07:09,200 --> 00:07:11,280
They were actually a venture capital backed startup.

103
00:07:11,360 --> 00:07:18,000
But still, I felt like I would learn more about the early stages of a company going there than going to Apple, which I think was the case.

104
00:07:18,800 --> 00:07:21,680
And that also allowed me to stay in the Midwest, which I wanted to do.

105
00:07:22,960 --> 00:07:25,040
And from there, did that for a while.

106
00:07:25,280 --> 00:07:26,960
I ended up at Garmin for a while.

107
00:07:27,760 --> 00:07:32,720
When I moved to the Minnesota area, Minneapolis area, my wife went to law school.

108
00:07:33,200 --> 00:07:39,360
So ended up working for a smaller offshoot of Garmin, working on kind of aviation, iPad apps.

109
00:07:39,600 --> 00:07:40,000
Oh, nice.

110
00:07:40,640 --> 00:07:41,360
Which was a lot of fun.

111
00:07:42,240 --> 00:07:42,800
That was really cool.

112
00:07:43,040 --> 00:07:48,160
A lot of fun to especially test it, because we had somebody there had a..

113
00:07:50,560 --> 00:07:59,280
fleet of planes for testing stuff, but here in the Minneapolis area, they had the kind of the owner of that subsidiary had a plane he would take us up in to test the iPad.

114
00:07:59,520 --> 00:08:00,560
Yeah, that's pretty awesome.

115
00:08:01,040 --> 00:08:01,440
That's cool.

116
00:08:02,320 --> 00:08:08,480
But from there, I ended up, you know, still new, I wanted to get into the entrepreneurship route.

117
00:08:08,720 --> 00:08:09,840
That was really where my heart was.

118
00:08:10,320 --> 00:08:11,920
Didn't have a great idea for it.

119
00:08:12,080 --> 00:08:13,520
Didn't really know how to get there still.

120
00:08:15,360 --> 00:08:18,080
But I decided to start doing some contracting.

121
00:08:19,280 --> 00:08:24,960
And so I left Garmin and did iOS app development.

122
00:08:24,960 --> 00:08:29,200
I worked with AWS app development as a contractor consultant and I really specialized in email apps.

123
00:08:29,760 --> 00:08:30,480
Oh, interesting.

124
00:08:30,960 --> 00:08:31,040
Yeah.

125
00:08:31,440 --> 00:08:44,480
And so years earlier, I had developed a framework called MailCore that made it really easy to work with iMap and SMTP and other email protocols.

126
00:08:45,440 --> 00:08:47,440
And this was started on the Mac.

127
00:08:47,920 --> 00:08:50,960
At the time, I was, I really wanted to build a Mac email client.

128
00:08:51,440 --> 00:08:55,600
I never got around to do it to be able to, but I did build out like the core of it.

129
00:08:55,760 --> 00:08:58,320
I built out like the framework in which you could build it on top of.

130
00:08:58,560 --> 00:09:00,240
And so I spun that out as open source.

131
00:09:00,880 --> 00:09:13,440
And over time, that became more and more popular, and especially became popular when the iPhone came onto the scene, because all of a sudden there was a lot more need for email clients written in Objective C.

132
00:09:13,840 --> 00:09:13,920
Yeah.

133
00:09:14,640 --> 00:09:16,320
And people wanted to do that on the iPhone.

134
00:09:16,480 --> 00:09:27,200
So I really had this business that was just focused on developing email apps and related things with email, and did that for a while.

135
00:09:28,400 --> 00:09:42,880
And that's actually how I got connected up with my co-founder again, because I saw that Giovanni had left Apple, and I'd always respected the world work he'd done and I had a really big project, a really large email client we needed to build.

136
00:09:43,200 --> 00:09:46,800
And I was like, I can't do this given the contracts I have on my own.

137
00:09:47,360 --> 00:09:48,480
I can't do this on my own.

138
00:09:49,840 --> 00:09:53,120
I wonder if Giovanni is available.

139
00:09:53,360 --> 00:09:54,480
So I contacted him on Twitter.

140
00:09:54,960 --> 00:09:55,200
Of course.

141
00:09:55,600 --> 00:09:56,880
Yeah, of course on Twitter, right?

142
00:09:57,200 --> 00:10:01,120
And he was like, Yeah, yeah, I'm trying to figure out what to do next.

143
00:10:01,360 --> 00:10:02,080
Yeah, this sounds great.

144
00:10:02,560 --> 00:10:03,600
Let's work together.

145
00:10:03,840 --> 00:10:08,720
And so we worked together for about six months and really enjoyed working together.

146
00:10:09,360 --> 00:10:14,800
We were still doing contracting, but we're like, you know, this is is so much fun.

147
00:10:15,920 --> 00:10:17,520
This is so much better than working solo.

148
00:10:18,240 --> 00:10:19,600
Like, let's make this an official thing.

149
00:10:19,760 --> 00:10:21,840
And so what we did is we set up a new company together.

150
00:10:22,240 --> 00:10:26,480
AstroHQ is the legal name of the company.

151
00:10:26,800 --> 00:10:28,320
So it was Astro from the start, okay.

152
00:10:28,640 --> 00:10:33,200
It was Astro from the start, yeah, and started working together in that company.

153
00:10:33,680 --> 00:10:39,680
He was in San Francisco, I was in Minneapolis, so we've always worked remote and worked on email apps together.

154
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:48,480
But both of us knew that while this was better because we were self employed and we did enjoy that more, we weren't the world's greatest contractors.

155
00:10:49,840 --> 00:10:50,800
We just didn't love it.

156
00:10:50,960 --> 00:10:52,560
We weren't great at getting new business.

157
00:10:53,360 --> 00:10:56,560
It just wasn't, it wasn't totally our cup of tea.

158
00:10:56,800 --> 00:11:02,640
So we knew that long term we wanted to hit into the product business, but we just still didn't know what kind of product to build.

159
00:11:03,280 --> 00:11:11,040
And that's where Astropat comes into the picture because we bounced for over a year.

160
00:11:11,680 --> 00:11:13,120
We bounced around different ideas.

161
00:11:13,520 --> 00:11:18,000
You know, like before we would work on our contract stuff, we'd have like, it was Skype at this time.

162
00:11:18,240 --> 00:11:19,680
This was before we used Zoom.

163
00:11:19,920 --> 00:11:22,640
We'd jump on Skype and be like, uh, you know, what ideas do you have?

164
00:11:22,720 --> 00:11:23,680
We bounced around ideas.

165
00:11:23,920 --> 00:11:26,960
And we just never had anything that really had legs to it.

166
00:11:27,520 --> 00:11:30,560
Because we wanted to find an idea that could support us both full time.

167
00:11:31,280 --> 00:11:32,160
Ideally, right?

168
00:11:32,400 --> 00:11:35,680
We didn't think we would be able to, we figured it would take some time to get there.

169
00:11:36,480 --> 00:11:40,480
But so many ideas got rejected for that reason.

170
00:11:40,480 --> 00:11:45,040
Because we're like, yeah, this is cool, but I think it's too small, too hard to make money on the App Store with this.

171
00:11:45,600 --> 00:11:46,720
Okay, we're not going to do it.

172
00:11:47,120 --> 00:11:52,880
And actually, we stayed totally away from the email space, even though we were pretty much experts in email at this point.

173
00:11:53,440 --> 00:11:57,360
Just because we knew how hard it was to compete with the built in and the free stuff.

174
00:11:57,520 --> 00:11:58,240
Like, ah, okay.

175
00:11:58,400 --> 00:12:02,640
It was well, there's probably some of that too.

176
00:12:03,280 --> 00:12:17,120
We were also it would be fun to work on something new, but we just didn't I had come around that if you were going to do your own email service, you were going to have to you were going to have to build out the server side component as well.

177
00:12:17,360 --> 00:12:18,240
Like what Hey did.

178
00:12:19,120 --> 00:12:20,320
Yeah, exactly, exactly.

179
00:12:20,480 --> 00:12:37,120
Hey is a great example of how even back then I think you'd, I was seeing you'd have to do it because this was after Sparrow, that great email app that was acquired by Google, and it just didn't look like you could, it was going to be really hard..

180
00:12:37,520 --> 00:12:39,600
It was going to be really hard to do that.

181
00:12:39,840 --> 00:12:42,640
Now, that's not to say that people aren't able to do it.

182
00:12:42,800 --> 00:12:45,120
There are, there's like Airmail, which has done a good job with it.

183
00:12:45,360 --> 00:12:48,880
The newer one, MimeStream, which is great for Gmail.

184
00:12:49,280 --> 00:12:53,200
So it can be done, but I we just knew we knew a little too much.

185
00:12:53,440 --> 00:13:03,200
We knew how difficult it was going to be and we as two people, neither of us with tons of back end web experience, we're like, nah, we don't really like, we don't want to run an email service in the back end for it.

186
00:13:03,360 --> 00:13:04,240
Like, we just, right.

187
00:13:04,560 --> 00:13:05,040
That's too much.

188
00:13:05,200 --> 00:13:06,800
That's too much to chew right now.

189
00:13:07,840 --> 00:13:09,040
So we looked for other ideas.

190
00:13:10,240 --> 00:13:20,000
And one day Giovanni went for a run and he ran past an ad in San Francisco for the Microsoft Surface.

191
00:13:20,560 --> 00:13:23,200
And on it, it had Photoshop and was showing it with a pen.

192
00:13:23,600 --> 00:13:26,320
And he came back and he was like, you know, that's so cool.

193
00:13:26,720 --> 00:13:33,040
Like, I we've both tried to use, you know, I've tried to use a Wacom tablet in the past and I had tried it, Wacom, Wacom, however you say it.

194
00:13:33,120 --> 00:13:33,760
Yeah, I'm not sure.

195
00:13:34,320 --> 00:13:35,360
Yeah, Wacom tablet.

196
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:37,120
And I had used one too.

197
00:13:37,440 --> 00:13:43,360
And we were both like, yeah, you know, we'd always wanted to use them to draw, but they were so frustrating to learn how to use.

198
00:13:44,240 --> 00:13:51,360
And then you did have the higher end Cintiq displays, the actual pen displays you could draw on, but those were priced so high.

199
00:13:51,760 --> 00:13:51,920
Right.

200
00:13:52,080 --> 00:13:55,280
It was only like the most pro artists could afford those.

201
00:13:55,440 --> 00:13:58,400
And we were amateurs at best, just playing around with this stuff.

202
00:13:58,560 --> 00:14:00,160
Like we couldn't justify something like that.

203
00:14:00,640 --> 00:14:05,280
And already the standard kind of black slate Wacom tablets, quite, quite expensive.

204
00:14:05,360 --> 00:14:06,480
I mean, a couple of hundred bucks.

205
00:14:06,960 --> 00:14:07,120
Yeah.

206
00:14:07,760 --> 00:14:11,920
We're like, you know, he was like, you know, is there a way to do this with an iPad?

207
00:14:12,400 --> 00:14:13,920
I was like, that's a great idea.

208
00:14:14,960 --> 00:14:15,680
Let's investigate.

209
00:14:16,400 --> 00:14:20,240
And that's what started the, the, the journey into AstraPad.

210
00:14:20,640 --> 00:14:24,720
That was where the initial product idea where, where it came from.

211
00:14:25,280 --> 00:14:29,360
And this was, this was before the iPad Pro and the Apple Pencil, right?

212
00:14:29,680 --> 00:14:30,720
Yes, this was way before.

213
00:14:30,880 --> 00:14:31,760
This is 2013.

214
00:14:32,240 --> 00:14:32,320
Okay.

215
00:14:33,200 --> 00:14:39,040
So we were making a bet that there were some third party styluses at the time.

216
00:14:39,120 --> 00:14:40,640
There was a Donut, was the big one.

217
00:14:41,200 --> 00:14:44,000
But they were all capacitive and they didn't have pressure sensitivity, right?

218
00:14:44,240 --> 00:14:44,560
That's right.

219
00:14:44,720 --> 00:14:44,800
Yeah.

220
00:14:44,960 --> 00:14:48,240
There was also, I don't know if Pencil by 53 was out yet or not.

221
00:14:48,400 --> 00:14:48,960
