And that proved to be a very good very good use of our time because that video essentially went viral when we launched because people were sharing it all over the place on social media like, hey, check this new use of an iPad out.

434
00:30:13,280 --> 00:30:13,760
Check this out.

435
00:30:13,920 --> 00:30:14,400
This is really neat.

436
00:30:14,640 --> 00:30:15,920
And people were just sharing the video.

437
00:30:16,640 --> 00:30:18,160
So we got tonnes of coverage from that.

438
00:30:18,400 --> 00:30:20,560
We got tonnes of press coverage as well from that.

439
00:30:20,800 --> 00:30:23,440
Just because it was something really new, something really fresh.

440
00:30:23,920 --> 00:30:26,480
We had a great video showing, showing it off.

441
00:30:27,200 --> 00:30:28,400
And so people shared that.

442
00:30:30,320 --> 00:30:35,920
We did have tonnes of new bugs, tonnes of new bugs that we had never seen before.

443
00:30:36,320 --> 00:30:45,360
Thankfully, you know, both of us at this point are really experienced on the Mac platform and iOS, so we were relatively quickly able to fix a lot of those.

444
00:30:46,880 --> 00:30:50,480
Have you had a sort of support system already set up?

445
00:30:50,960 --> 00:31:01,680
No, it was just a Gmail inbox at that point, just like that was the extent of our support, just like email support, and it went to a Gmail inbox, which within a couple of months, we quickly outgrown.

446
00:31:02,080 --> 00:31:02,880
Yeah, I imagine.

447
00:31:03,520 --> 00:31:12,720
I guess it's interesting, like, on the one hand, it's like overwhelming when I think about what you had to do in terms of how close to the metal you had to get on everything.

448
00:31:13,840 --> 00:31:32,400
But then, the more I'm thinking about it, like, anything I've ever done that is pseudo unique in terms of interactions with different pieces of hardware, software, part of what makes support so hard is that I'm sitting at the top and I'm hooking together all these complicated things, but you were actually building the lower level thing.

449
00:31:32,560 --> 00:31:43,280
So I bet it actually helped, like you could probably identify and fix problems almost faster because you weren't trying to learn, you know, what is Apple doing here in core animation that could be causing this to happen?

450
00:31:43,600 --> 00:31:44,560
Oh, yeah, that's true.

451
00:31:44,720 --> 00:31:45,360
That is very true.

452
00:31:45,360 --> 00:31:46,320
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

453
00:31:46,560 --> 00:31:47,680
That it was very, very.

454
00:31:48,000 --> 00:31:51,680
So, pretty much for us, we'd get bugs in two places.

455
00:31:53,040 --> 00:31:54,160
Well, three places.

456
00:31:55,200 --> 00:32:08,160
It would be the input system when you would draw, like rendering out the drawing on the Max side of things as you draw in the iPad, making sure the pressure was right, making sure the tilt and all those different things were right.

457
00:32:08,640 --> 00:32:10,000
We spent a lot of time working on that.

458
00:32:11,280 --> 00:32:17,600
Another area was when we'd hit the GPU, because our liquid codec as well is partially GPU accelerated.

459
00:32:17,760 --> 00:32:17,840
Okay.

460
00:32:18,240 --> 00:32:21,040
So we have some special kernels that run on the GPU.

461
00:32:22,320 --> 00:32:26,880
And once you get that low level, you start to run into GPU specific issues.

462
00:32:27,120 --> 00:32:42,480
So that is the one downside of being that low level is the hardware becomes less abstracted and you have to look at more like per, okay, we need to get that exact model Mac with that exact model GPU to reproduce this problem.

463
00:32:42,800 --> 00:32:49,680
I guess that's another advantage though to going Apple only because the amount of SKUs we have to support is just so much less.

464
00:32:50,480 --> 00:32:50,880
Definitely.

465
00:32:51,440 --> 00:32:53,760
And it was already really demanding on our team at that point.

466
00:32:54,000 --> 00:32:59,520
And then the other big class of bugs that still we really struggle with this to this day is wifi bugs.

467
00:33:00,640 --> 00:33:04,320
But that's really, really hard because it often comes down to like the WiFi router they're using.

468
00:33:04,480 --> 00:33:06,480
It's like, well, you don't have a great WiFi router.

469
00:33:06,880 --> 00:33:10,240
So many settings and Yeah, there's configured weird ways and yeah.

470
00:33:10,560 --> 00:33:12,640
So that that gets to be pretty tough.

471
00:33:13,120 --> 00:33:17,920
But yeah, we we are working at a much, much lower level generally.

472
00:33:18,240 --> 00:33:18,480
Nice.

473
00:33:18,640 --> 00:33:22,880
So, so the launch went well, seemed like it was attracting a lot of attention.

474
00:33:23,280 --> 00:33:30,000
Um, in, you know, business you speak, it's, it seems like there was definitely product market fit in terms of Yeah, definitely, definitely.

475
00:33:30,080 --> 00:33:31,120
The launch told us it was there.

476
00:33:31,440 --> 00:34:17,920
Yeah, I clearly this was as that one anecdote from the guy from Pixar, I think, foretold it's like yes there's something special when you see it you're like oh my gosh this thing i know and this thing i use can connect in this way and it just like unlocks all these ideas uh-huh uh-huh um yeah totally so at some point you switched over to a subscription model yep what drove that and how did that go yeah so well early on also did a with our early launch while we had a ton of eyeballs on it financially it wasn't it was good but it wasn't outstanding um and we had a period after that where you know it wasn'tt clear if we were going to be able to keep doing this to be able to sustain the two of us.

477
00:34:18,080 --> 00:34:18,240
Right.

478
00:34:18,720 --> 00:34:20,000
Putting all your time into it.

479
00:34:20,640 --> 00:34:21,040
Exactly.

480
00:34:21,200 --> 00:34:21,200
Yeah.

481
00:34:21,760 --> 00:34:24,720
So we this was a couple months after launch.

482
00:34:25,040 --> 00:34:33,040
The excitement of the launch had died down and looking at kind of day to day, month to month sales, it was like, I don't know if there's enough here to sustain this.

483
00:34:33,440 --> 00:34:36,160
But at this point, we weren't doing contracting.

484
00:34:36,560 --> 00:34:42,320
We decided to go all in and we used the money we had gotten from the launch to pay ourselves.

485
00:34:42,560 --> 00:34:45,600
I think I forgot how many months we could go.

486
00:34:45,680 --> 00:34:47,600
It was like four months, something like that that we could go.

487
00:34:47,680 --> 00:34:55,120
And we're like, okay, we're going to use this time because, again, this is a huge opportunity that doesn't come along pretty much.

488
00:34:55,360 --> 00:34:55,440
Yeah.

489
00:34:55,920 --> 00:34:57,360
So we need to go after this.

490
00:34:57,840 --> 00:35:00,800
And so the first thing that helped was switching to the App Store.

491
00:35:01,360 --> 00:35:02,960
That helped bring things around as well.

492
00:35:03,360 --> 00:35:21,360
And then we continued to do improvements to the app, but then it became clear that we were also pretty limited by the upfront pricing because it's all things considered, it's still a relatively small market because you need somebody that has a Mac, you need somebody that has at that time an iPad Pro.

493
00:35:22,960 --> 00:35:30,960
Well, actually at that point the iPad Pro is wasn't even out, but later they would need a Mac, they needed an iPad Pro, they needed an Apple Pencil, like there's not a ton of people.

494
00:35:31,440 --> 00:35:35,920
And be somebody who would want a tablet for drawing.

495
00:35:36,240 --> 00:35:37,200
Yeah, exactly, exactly.

496
00:35:37,440 --> 00:35:41,200
So we knew we really had to raise our prices to make this sustainable long term.

497
00:35:41,920 --> 00:35:47,680
And we knew that with because of what we the App Store that the App Store really drags down prices.

498
00:35:47,920 --> 00:35:51,840
And even what we were charging at that time, thirty dollars was exceptionally high.

499
00:35:52,160 --> 00:35:52,720
Yeah, yeah.

500
00:35:53,680 --> 00:35:54,960
for an iPad app.

501
00:35:55,760 --> 00:36:03,120
And so we knew that if we were going to be able to get like professional class pricing, like we weren't going to be able to able to do it with upfront pricing.

502
00:36:03,680 --> 00:36:12,160
So we knew we had to raise our price and we knew we had to raise our price to continue development and be able to support ourselves long term and build a team around it so we could make the product better as well.

503
00:36:13,280 --> 00:36:15,120
To make this whole thing sustainable.

504
00:36:16,240 --> 00:36:22,560
So about the same time subscription pricing just started to really come onto the App Store.

505
00:36:22,720 --> 00:36:25,040
This was when was this?

506
00:36:25,360 --> 00:36:28,320
twenty seventeen, twenty sixteen, I think we were working on it.

507
00:36:28,560 --> 00:36:28,640
Okay.

508
00:36:29,440 --> 00:36:35,600
And Apple just announced there were still some restrictions around it, but they were starting to open up the subscription pricing for apps.

509
00:36:35,680 --> 00:36:35,840
Yeah.

510
00:36:36,080 --> 00:36:38,640
Yeah, because it used to be just like news apps or something.

511
00:36:39,520 --> 00:36:39,920
Just like services.

512
00:36:40,560 --> 00:36:42,400
You really had to have a strong service component.

513
00:36:43,280 --> 00:36:44,640
So we saw this as an opportunity.

514
00:36:44,960 --> 00:36:48,560
We're like, you know, everything appears to be moving towards subscription.

515
00:36:49,040 --> 00:36:57,440
This seems to be a really good way to sell a professional class, a professional creative tool.

516
00:36:57,760 --> 00:36:59,040
This is what Adobe has been doing.

517
00:36:59,280 --> 00:37:02,640
This is what everyone else in the creative space is moving towards.

518
00:37:03,440 --> 00:37:05,920
I think it makes sense for us to move towards this as well.

519
00:37:07,200 --> 00:37:15,600
And then it also makes it more accessible too, where if you only want to use it for a couple of months, you can just pay for a cou couple months pricing because we have a lot of people that do project based work.

520
00:37:16,000 --> 00:37:17,440
They can just pay for it a couple months.

521
00:37:18,080 --> 00:37:20,240
If you want to use it more, you can pay for the annual.

522
00:37:20,720 --> 00:37:27,920
And this allows us to charge a higher price, but still a price that's fair for our customers and is going to allow us to sustain it long term.

523
00:37:28,320 --> 00:37:34,000
So we knew this was something we had to switch to, and we spent about a year working up towards it.

524
00:37:34,640 --> 00:37:39,040
We developed a completely new spin off of Astropad.

525
00:37:39,280 --> 00:37:41,680
It used the same core tech, but it had way more features.

526
00:37:41,920 --> 00:37:53,760
We essentially took all the amazing feature ideas that people had from using our first version of A of Astropad and brought it into what we call Astropad Studio, and that's our subscription version.

527
00:37:54,000 --> 00:37:54,080
Okay.

528
00:37:54,480 --> 00:38:01,840
And so it had things like magic gestures, which is a way of using your fingers to control what the pencil is doing.

529
00:38:02,160 --> 00:38:04,960
So if you use just the pencil alone, it draws.

530
00:38:05,200 --> 00:38:07,440
You can do one finger down and it can do a different action.

531
00:38:07,600 --> 00:38:10,720
So by default we have it set to an eraser or two fingers.

532
00:38:10,880 --> 00:38:13,840
That could be by default it's right click or three fingers.

533
00:38:14,000 --> 00:38:15,520
You can do multiple fingers at the same time.

534
00:38:15,920 --> 00:38:18,480
So that was a really cool feature we added.

535
00:38:18,720 --> 00:38:24,720
Was that as I feel like at this point that's almost like a convention for drawing apps on the iPad.

536
00:38:25,280 --> 00:38:26,000
Was that at that point?

537
00:38:26,960 --> 00:38:28,480
Yeah, this was totally new at that point.

538
00:38:28,640 --> 00:38:29,680
Okay, that's interesting.

539
00:38:29,840 --> 00:38:29,920
Yeah.

540
00:38:30,400 --> 00:38:36,640
Because it's very intuitive when you're using, I mean, like even Photoshop, you know, just recently came over to the iPad.

541
00:38:37,120 --> 00:38:45,040
It's not quite as intuitive as others, but it has the concept of, you know, you're using your fingers to hit a button to it's like the equivalent of your modifier keys.

542
00:38:45,760 --> 00:38:46,320
Yeah, yeah.

543
00:38:46,560 --> 00:38:49,040
And we were one of the first to do it if not the first to do it.

544
00:38:49,200 --> 00:38:49,600
That's cool.

545
00:38:50,960 --> 00:38:53,040
I think we were the first, but, you know, hard to say.

546
00:38:53,440 --> 00:38:56,560
Yeah, it could have been multiple people coming out with the same thing at the same time.

547
00:38:57,040 --> 00:38:58,240
The same idea at the same time, right?

548
00:38:58,800 --> 00:39:00,240
But that was a pretty big deal for us.

549
00:39:00,240 --> 00:39:08,160
For us, there wasn't a ton, like we were pretty early on in both app subscriptions and doing stuff with the Apple Pencil.

550
00:39:08,400 --> 00:39:08,480
Yeah.

551
00:39:08,880 --> 00:39:13,200
So this was a lot of new territory we were breaking with Astropad Studio.

552
00:39:14,080 --> 00:39:21,760
I remember at the time, I think it was Procreate really pioneered the idea of two finger tap to undo, three finger tap to redo.

553
00:39:22,000 --> 00:39:23,440
So we adopted that convention as well.

554
00:39:23,520 --> 00:39:28,240
So they're another one that today that seems like so obvious, but that wasn't always the thing either.

555
00:39:28,400 --> 00:39:29,680
Like someone had to come up with that.

556
00:39:29,920 --> 00:39:36,720
I feel like around that time, I don't know if it was because of the Pro or it just happened to be around the same time, maybe a little before.

557
00:39:37,600 --> 00:39:38,880
Kind of like what you were talking about before.

558
00:39:39,040 --> 00:39:43,200
At first with the iPad, it wasn't a very much of a tool.

559
00:39:43,360 --> 00:39:44,560
It was like a consumption device.

560
00:39:44,640 --> 00:39:46,080
That was always how we would refer to it, right?

561
00:39:46,320 --> 00:39:49,040
This is the consumption device and the Mac is the production device.

562
00:39:49,520 --> 00:40:07,920
And it feels like there was a crop of innovation happening all around this time with you and with Procreate and a couple others that we're really starting to like play with how do we use this totally different input paradigm to get similar creative results?

563
00:40:09,360 --> 00:40:12,240
It's really fun as someone who didn't live through the early days of the Mac.

564
00:40:13,200 --> 00:40:16,080
When I came on the scene, it's like these were all conventions that were set in stone.

565
00:40:16,480 --> 00:40:19,120
It was fun to get to like witness those being kind of born.

566
00:40:19,920 --> 00:40:25,120
Yeah, no, that was super and it was super fun to experiment with that and be able to build on that.

567
00:40:25,280 --> 00:40:29,760
And it's flattering too to see apps adopt now similar things too.

568
00:40:30,160 --> 00:40:36,800
Like one of the signature things we'd have in Astrapad was this like ring that you'd move around.

569
00:40:37,360 --> 00:40:44,000
And this ring was a way of it was also kind of like a modifier and we could show and hide a sidebar that was something new.

570
00:40:44,320 --> 00:40:46,000
And now you'll see other apps with that.

571
00:40:46,320 --> 00:40:47,680
Yeah, they're drawing apps with that too.

572
00:40:47,840 --> 00:40:49,440
Photoshop even has something similar.

573
00:40:49,600 --> 00:40:51,200
It's like a ring as a modifier.

574
00:40:51,520 --> 00:40:54,400
And that's something we also came up with with Astropad as well.

575
00:40:54,640 --> 00:40:55,280
That's awesome.

576
00:40:56,000 --> 00:41:00,160
And so we put all this stuff together and that was Astropad Studio.

577
00:41:00,400 --> 00:41:03,520
And that was our professional version of Astropad.

578
00:41:04,480 --> 00:41:06,960
And that's what we started charging a subscription for.

579
00:41:07,280 --> 00:41:07,520
Nice.

580
00:41:07,680 --> 00:41:13,280
And that, I mean, you obviously are still around as a business and you have employees.

581
00:41:13,440 --> 00:41:15,760
So I'm assuming that that seems to go well.

582
00:41:16,720 --> 00:41:18,080
Yeah, it did really well.

583
00:41:18,880 --> 00:41:24,880
It was quite nerve-wracking for about six months because we saw our revenues go down dramatically.

584
00:41:25,520 --> 00:41:25,680
Right.

585
00:41:26,000 --> 00:41:34,320
This was as expected, but still, like as part of the switch to the subscription, you're switching from a lot less advance payments to a lot more like monthly payments that are going to build up over time.

586
00:41:34,560 --> 00:41:46,160
So that was quite nerve-racking, but by about six months in, we could see it was working and that our revenue was going up and we were seeing a really bright future for Astropad.

587
00:41:46,560 --> 00:41:47,120
That's awesome.

588
00:41:47,680 --> 00:41:54,320
And so at what point did you mention it earlier, but you have another product called Luna Display.

589
00:41:54,960 --> 00:41:57,040
That is a it's a actual hardware.

590
00:41:57,200 --> 00:41:59,680
It's literally a piece of hardware that you send to customers.

591
00:41:59,920 --> 00:42:00,000
Yep.

592
00:42:00,800 --> 00:42:04,880
And they can use that to mirror their Mac on their iPad.

593
00:42:05,120 --> 00:42:07,760
So kind of going in, wait, yeah, I guess I said that right.

594
00:42:08,160 --> 00:42:09,920
Well, you can use it as a second display.

595
00:42:10,480 --> 00:42:12,320
So you can use, right, yeah, yeah.

596
00:42:12,880 --> 00:42:14,320
Second display for your, for your Mac.

597
00:42:14,720 --> 00:42:16,480
Not mirroring, because you wouldn't draw on your Mac.

598
00:42:16,960 --> 00:42:19,200
Yeah, Astropad mirrors, Astropad mirrors.

599
00:42:19,440 --> 00:42:22,880
So when did, when did this idea sort of form?

600
00:42:23,360 --> 00:42:26,720
And then how did you, how did you get across the finish line for making a real physical product?

601
00:42:27,040 --> 00:42:28,320
Yeah, so that was pretty intense.

602
00:42:28,480 --> 00:42:32,640
That was like another, that was like our Astropad 1.0 journey all over again.

603
00:42:33,680 --> 00:42:36,560
Well, how it came about was a lot of people really.

604
00:42:37,360 --> 00:42:43,440
wanted to use the while they were using Astropad, they really wanted it also to act as a second display.

605
00:42:43,760 --> 00:42:48,720
So while they were drawing, they could get extra screen real estate, not just mirror, because Astropad is always mirrored.

606
00:42:48,960 --> 00:42:49,120
Right.

607
00:42:49,840 --> 00:42:50,960
What's on your main display.

608
00:42:51,760 --> 00:43:07,600
And I mean, this made a ton of sense to us, and we looked into how you could do it, and there was no APIs or there was no official way to do this, to be able to create a virtual second display through software only.

609
00:43:08,320 --> 00:43:14,080
There were some hacks out there, there were some kernel extensions, but they had major, major, major differences Yeah.

610
00:43:14,400 --> 00:43:16,640
And to build a product on that that you need to support long term.

611
00:43:17,200 --> 00:43:17,280
Yeah.

612
00:43:17,520 --> 00:43:18,160
That's scary.

613
00:43:18,240 --> 00:43:18,320
Yeah.

614
00:43:18,960 --> 00:43:20,640
They were using deprecated APIs.

615
00:43:21,200 --> 00:43:24,080
They didn't work right with GPU acceleration.

616
00:43:24,560 --> 00:43:27,760
And remember, a bunch of our tech is GPU accelerated.

617
00:43:27,920 --> 00:43:34,720
And on top of that, when you're working in a creative program like Photoshop or a 3D program, I mean, you're really relying on the GPU.

618
00:43:35,120 --> 00:43:35,280
Right.

619
00:43:35,840 --> 00:43:37,440
To do a lot of that crunching for you.

620
00:43:37,600 --> 00:43:38,480
So that was really no go.

621
00:43:38,640 --> 00:43:40,400
So we were like, okay, how can we make this work?

622
00:43:40,640 --> 00:43:44,720
And we heard of people using, gosh, I forgot what they're called.

623
00:43:44,960 --> 00:43:48,560
Like dummy dongles where you plug it in.

624
00:43:49,040 --> 00:43:55,280
for, say, you have a Mac mini in a server room and you plug in this little dongle to show up as a monitor.

625
00:43:55,920 --> 00:43:57,280
We're like, oh, that's pretty interesting.

626
00:43:57,440 --> 00:43:59,200
So we got some of these and we played around with them.

627
00:43:59,360 --> 00:44:01,280
We're like, huh, this is pretty neat.

628
00:44:01,840 --> 00:44:05,760
You can plug these in and all of a sudden an extra screen shows up on your Mac.

629
00:44:06,160 --> 00:44:07,200
Okay, that's pretty neat.

630
00:44:08,880 --> 00:44:12,400
But they were always hardcoded to some like arbitrary screen resolution.

631
00:44:13,040 --> 00:44:14,160
They would never match the iPad.

632
00:44:14,400 --> 00:44:16,720
So we're like, okay, this is pretty interesting though.

633
00:44:16,960 --> 00:44:17,680
What if we could do this?

634
00:44:17,840 --> 00:44:23,040
What if we made our own that we could program to match whatever screen size and resolution we wanted?

635
00:44:23,600 --> 00:44:25,120
Then we could use that with Astroropad.

636
00:44:25,440 --> 00:44:29,920
And so what you could do is you could plug this hardware piece into your Mac.

637
00:44:30,320 --> 00:44:31,440
You'd get an extra screen.

638
00:44:32,000 --> 00:44:34,000
You'd get full GPU acceleration.

639
00:44:34,320 --> 00:44:36,800
It would act like a regular monitor does.

640
00:44:37,440 --> 00:44:44,960
No hacks, no weird gotchas, just the full experience of a second display, and then we can stream it to the iPad.

641
00:44:46,000 --> 00:44:52,160
And so this really started out as something we wanted to do for Astropad to make that as like a new Astropad feature.

642
00:44:53,440 --> 00:45:01,920
But pretty quickly into developing this hardware, we had a contact from our time at Apple who worked on the hardware side of things at Apple.

643
00:45:02,960 --> 00:45:09,760
And he had also gone off and was doing his own company, but he kind of pointed us in the right direction to get started and helped us.

644
00:45:10,320 --> 00:45:16,480
Their company actually helped us with the early prototypes of our LUNA Display, what would eventually become our LUNA Display hardware.

645
00:45:17,360 --> 00:45:19,760
And so we started prototyping that.

646
00:45:20,400 --> 00:45:25,840
And as we were prototyping it, we realized that this was just a fantastic product in its own right.

647
00:45:26,400 --> 00:45:28,160
We're like, this is so useful.

648
00:45:28,800 --> 00:45:31,120
And it's useful in completely different ways.

649
00:45:31,680 --> 00:45:32,400
Completely different ways.

650
00:45:32,720 --> 00:45:36,160
Astropad, who was targeted very specifically to creative uses.

651
00:45:37,440 --> 00:45:43,520
And this was just we just thought this was fantastic and we're like, okay, this can no longer just be a feature for Astropad.

652
00:45:43,840 --> 00:45:47,520
Because we were really trying to come up with feature parity with like a Wacom Cintiq, right?

653
00:45:47,760 --> 00:45:49,040
Like we can do everything they can do.

654
00:45:49,360 --> 00:45:52,080
And of course, one of the things they can do is act as a second display.

655
00:45:52,160 --> 00:45:54,000
So we wanted Astropad to be able to do that too.

656
00:45:54,800 --> 00:45:58,400
Well, now we're like, we've got this other awesome potential product in our hands.

657
00:45:59,040 --> 00:46:04,160
So not only should this work with Astropad, but we should really make this be its own standalone product too.

658
00:46:04,720 --> 00:46:06,160
And that became Luna Display.

659
00:46:06,800 --> 00:46:11,680
And we put a prototype together and then we did a Kickstarter for it.

660
00:46:12,240 --> 00:46:13,440
That was huge for us.

661
00:46:14,000 --> 00:46:15,600
What was the reason for Kickstarter?

662
00:46:16,000 --> 00:46:20,320
Well, it was we still felt like the idea of lunar display was still kind of crazy.

663
00:46:21,040 --> 00:46:23,760
We're like, is anyone going to pay for this, right?

664
00:46:24,240 --> 00:46:26,080
Like, is anyone actually going to buy this?

665
00:46:26,720 --> 00:46:36,560
We think it's super cool, we think it's awesome, we think it's a great product, we think it also makes AstraPad way better, but does anyone else feel that way, right?

666
00:46:36,800 --> 00:46:37,920
Do we have a product market fit?

667
00:46:38,960 --> 00:46:52,880
And we thought Kickstarter was a great way to do that because while we had a prototype, manufacturing this thing was going to be a whole new level, a whole new level of pain, a whole new level of difficultyy, whole new challenges, cost a ton of money as well.

668
00:46:53,360 --> 00:46:57,200
And we didn't want to go in and do that unless we could actually prove that people wanted this.

669
00:46:57,360 --> 00:47:06,480
Yeah, it would be terrifying to invest that much money not knowing if anyone cared, or if it was just fun to share on Twitter, but you wouldn't actually buy it.

670
00:47:06,960 --> 00:47:14,080
Yeah, yeah, and if we were not going to be able to get the scale, if there wasn't going to be enough people interested, we were just not, we were just going to abandon the project at that point.

671
00:47:14,400 --> 00:47:14,560
Right.

672
00:47:15,680 --> 00:47:19,600
We just, yeah, we needed to make sure there was enough interest there.

673
00:47:20,000 --> 00:47:23,040
It's also taking time away from you improving Estropad, right?

674
00:47:23,360 --> 00:47:31,120
Yeah, ton of time., tons of time away, and it was super, super difficult, because we weren't people with hardware experience.

675
00:47:31,280 --> 00:47:39,840
So there was a lot we had to learn, which was fun, because it really pushed our boundaries and we learned a ton about hardware products and hardware design and manufacturing.

676
00:47:40,640 --> 00:47:43,760
But yeah, it was a lot, it was a real lot.

677
00:47:44,320 --> 00:47:46,560
But that changed when we did our Kickstarter.

678
00:47:47,040 --> 00:47:53,760
And our Kickstarter was a massive success for us, way beyond anything we had anticipated.

679
00:47:54,240 --> 00:47:56,240
I'm trying to remember what the original amount was.

680
00:47:56,400 --> 00:47:59,200
I think it was like 650,000, I want to say.

681
00:47:59,280 --> 00:48:00,880
Oh yeah, it's probably still there on there.

682
00:48:00,960 --> 00:48:02,240
I'll link to it in the show notes.

683
00:48:02,960 --> 00:48:07,760
Obviously, similar approach to making a slick video to show.

684
00:48:08,560 --> 00:48:09,280
Same thing, yeah.

685
00:48:09,840 --> 00:48:13,680
Which fits perfectly with the whole ethos of Kickstarter, right?

686
00:48:14,080 --> 00:48:14,480
Absolutely.

687
00:48:14,640 --> 00:48:16,000
So we did the whole Kickstarter thing.

688
00:48:16,160 --> 00:48:20,160
We did a slick video, did a nice campaign, put it up there.

689
00:48:21,760 --> 00:48:25,280
Much like our first AstroPed launch, we sought out TechPress.

690
00:48:26,160 --> 00:48:29,600
One thing we did that worked really well too is we shipped demo units.

691
00:48:30,320 --> 00:48:32,400
We had prototype, little prototype units.

692
00:48:32,720 --> 00:48:35,280
They were really, really rough.

693
00:48:35,520 --> 00:48:40,400
They just look like a raw circuit board, but we ship those actually to journalists.

694
00:48:40,800 --> 00:48:42,480
And so they're like, oh, this is pretty neat.

695
00:48:42,640 --> 00:48:44,000
And so they plug it in and play with it.

696
00:48:44,240 --> 00:48:45,600
And we got great coverage through that.

697
00:48:45,840 --> 00:48:46,320
Oh, yeah.

698
00:48:46,480 --> 00:48:47,120
That's a good idea.

699
00:48:47,280 --> 00:48:49,520
And like, they're going to be understanding of the fact that it's a...

700
00:48:50,080 --> 00:48:50,160
Yeah.

701
00:48:50,320 --> 00:48:51,600
An ugly prototype thing, right?

702
00:48:51,920 --> 00:48:58,400
Well, because it was so I'll have to dig up a picture of it, but it was so obviously prototype that people were willing to cut us some slack as well.

703
00:48:59,360 --> 00:49:01,120
And they were just going to see the potential in it.

704
00:49:01,200 --> 00:49:03,680
They're like, you know, this is working really well for a prototype.

705
00:49:03,840 --> 00:49:04,000
You know?

706
00:49:04,160 --> 00:49:09,360
And then it was also hard for them to ignore because it was not often that they were getting in the mail prototype hardware.

707
00:49:09,520 --> 00:49:11,200
So they're like, wow, this is pretty unique.

708
00:49:11,360 --> 00:49:11,360
Okay.

709
00:49:11,600 --> 00:49:12,000
What is this?

710
00:49:12,000 --> 00:49:12,960
I'm going to check this out.

711
00:49:14,240 --> 00:49:23,680
And that worked really well to generate buzz and excitement around Astropath or Lunar Display and really got the campaign off to a great start.

712
00:49:24,400 --> 00:49:32,240
So at that point then, and maybe before this, you really had to start thinking about how do we actually make these at scale?

713
00:49:33,600 --> 00:49:35,120
Yeah, that was pretty daunting.

714
00:49:36,960 --> 00:49:40,640
It hurts my brain to think about how kind of scary that would be.

715
00:49:41,600 --> 00:49:43,920
Yeah, because we'd heard so many horror stories too.

716
00:49:44,560 --> 00:49:47,840
Because we got connected with others that had done Kickstarter campaigns.

717
00:49:48,800 --> 00:49:57,200
And then again, we also had some help from our friend that had done hardware and was doing his own hardware startup and had worked at hardware at Apple.

718
00:49:57,680 --> 00:50:01,680
So he knew a couple of things about, you know, could kind of point us in the right direction.

719
00:50:02,720 --> 00:50:07,520
But still, it was, it was, it was hard.

720
00:50:08,160 --> 00:50:20,320
We really lucked out and in the end we got connected with someone that he's kind of like a liaison between us and a factory actually where they're manufactured in China.

721
00:50:20,560 --> 00:50:21,840
And he has just been awesome.

722
00:50:22,720 --> 00:50:35,360
And we really lucked out because we'd heard so many horror horror stories about getting things manufactured and factories not doing it right and having to switch factories and just being a very complex process.

723
00:50:35,920 --> 00:50:36,000
Yeah.

724
00:50:36,960 --> 00:50:47,360
Thankfully, we ran into someone who was just it was like a word of mouth referral, I think, that we got connected with this person that had been manufacturing some other products.

725
00:50:48,640 --> 00:50:49,840
Giovanni, he went over there.

726
00:50:50,000 --> 00:50:50,720
He flew over to China.

727
00:50:51,040 --> 00:50:54,320
He was there multiple times and met them and went to the factory.

728
00:50:55,280 --> 00:50:57,920
And at the factory went through multiple rounds of things.

729
00:50:58,000 --> 00:51:00,160
That was where they figured out, like, how are we going to do the plastics?

730
00:51:00,640 --> 00:51:03,040
How are we going to do certification?

731
00:51:03,200 --> 00:51:05,920
How are we going to do all these aspects of a hardware product?

732
00:51:06,880 --> 00:51:14,000
And thankfully, thanks to our, the person we, that was in, he was actually in Hong Kong that we dealt with.

733
00:51:14,320 --> 00:51:22,560
He just did such a phenomenal job that that took a lot of the risk out of it for us, and we were able to deliver our Kickstarter campaign.

734
00:51:22,800 --> 00:51:24,640
We were six months late on it.

735
00:51:25,120 --> 00:51:27,520
Which is like ahead of schedule in Kickstarter time, right?

736
00:51:27,840 --> 00:51:27,920
Yeah.

737
00:51:29,600 --> 00:51:36,320
We were really hopeful we were going to be able to do it on time, but it became that supporting all the different max out there because we had.

738
00:51:36,320 --> 00:51:41,200
We had USB C and we had a mini display port version.

739
00:51:41,520 --> 00:51:45,200
And the mini display port version in particular gave us a lot of headaches.

740
00:51:46,560 --> 00:51:52,800
And so we ended up having to test across pretty much every mini display Mac we could get our hands on.

741
00:51:53,760 --> 00:51:54,480
Do you still sell that one?

742
00:51:54,960 --> 00:51:55,440
Yeah, we do.

743
00:51:55,760 --> 00:51:55,840
Okay.

744
00:51:56,640 --> 00:51:56,720
Yeah.

745
00:51:56,960 --> 00:52:00,160
It's a lot less popular these days, but we still sell it and people buy it.

746
00:52:00,480 --> 00:52:00,560
Yeah.

747
00:52:00,720 --> 00:52:06,560
I mean, there's I imagine there's still a lot of a lot of inventory out there, or not inventory, but one's in use.

748
00:52:06,960 --> 00:52:07,040
Yeah.

749
00:52:07,280 --> 00:52:07,360
Yeah.

750
00:52:07,680 --> 00:52:07,760
Yeah.

751
00:52:08,000 --> 00:52:08,000
Yeah.

752
00:52:08,320 --> 00:52:08,400
Yeah.

753
00:52:08,560 --> 00:52:10,800
There's a lot of a lot of old, old Macs out there.

754
00:52:11,280 --> 00:52:12,640
And we added a feature too later.

755
00:52:12,640 --> 00:52:18,400
We have a function too, later, we call it Mac to Mac, where you can use an old Mac as a display as well, and so that's been really popular too.

756
00:52:18,720 --> 00:52:28,320
Yeah, especially as iMacs have lost target display mode, and so once you don't want to use that computer, it's just a giant, beautiful, unusable monitor.

757
00:52:29,360 --> 00:52:35,200
Yeah, and so, yeah, that's definitely, I think, one of the cool use cases for Lunar Display.

758
00:52:36,400 --> 00:52:48,400
So did you initially launch worldwide or like, because unlike the App Store where so much of that gets nicely abstracted away from you, you have to deal with regulatory bodies, shipments, all this other stuff, right?

759
00:52:49,360 --> 00:52:51,120
So how did you handle that?

760
00:52:51,360 --> 00:52:52,320
Yeah, you're totally right.

761
00:52:52,560 --> 00:52:52,560
Yeah.

762
00:52:53,600 --> 00:52:53,680
Yeah.

763
00:52:54,000 --> 00:52:55,600
So we did have a limited rollout.

764
00:52:56,000 --> 00:53:13,920
We do support more countries now, but it was pretty much, I want to say the EU and the US, Canada, trying to remember where else, but that was roughly where we started because yeah, there's different certification bodies for electronics as well.

765
00:53:14,400 --> 00:53:17,680
Thankfully, our partner in China helped us with that getting certified.

766
00:53:17,920 --> 00:53:22,240
And so we have the proper certifications for the different countries.

767
00:53:23,040 --> 00:53:24,240
And then also.

768
00:53:24,400 --> 00:53:31,120
Also, via a friend that did a Kickstarter, we got connected to a great third party fulfillment service.

769
00:53:31,600 --> 00:53:32,000
Ah, okay.

770
00:53:32,800 --> 00:53:33,920
And I handled that.

771
00:53:34,080 --> 00:53:38,240
I had to fly down to Atlanta where they're based, meet with them, get things set up.

772
00:53:38,880 --> 00:53:43,120
And they handled the fulfillment of our initial Kickstarter.

773
00:53:43,360 --> 00:53:46,480
And really, ever since then, they've handled our fulfillment.

774
00:53:47,120 --> 00:53:51,520
And they are able to ship, you know, to the US and to most countries in the world.

775
00:53:53,280 --> 00:53:58,400
But that would have been exceptionally difficult had we not found the right people to help us.

776
00:53:58,640 --> 00:54:00,400
And it's getting easier all the time.

777
00:54:01,520 --> 00:54:05,840
because of the rise of like Shopify and a lot of people doing more e commerce like that.

778
00:54:06,560 --> 00:54:07,840
It's getting easier all the time.

779
00:54:08,480 --> 00:54:10,640
And there's more of these third party fulfillment services.

780
00:54:11,680 --> 00:54:14,800
But on our own, on our own, it would have been next to impossible.

781
00:54:15,200 --> 00:54:15,280
Yeah.

782
00:54:15,520 --> 00:54:19,760
It's because you were still, how many employees did you have when you launched Luna?

783
00:54:20,160 --> 00:54:23,120
I mean, I, not an exact number, but about ten.

784
00:54:23,680 --> 00:54:23,760
Okay.

785
00:54:23,920 --> 00:54:26,560
So you were, well, eight, maybe, eight.

786
00:54:27,040 --> 00:54:29,120
Well, yeah, I would say eight, maybe.

787
00:54:29,280 --> 00:54:29,360
Yeah.

788
00:54:29,760 --> 00:54:29,840
Okay.

789
00:54:30,240 --> 00:54:30,320
Yeah.

790
00:54:30,480 --> 00:54:31,200
So we had grown.

791
00:54:31,440 --> 00:54:49,840
We had some more, we had some contract hardware people that were helping us with the Luna design., we had some software engineers on the team marketing and support was really, and then me and Giovanni and we were really using Astropad Studio.

792
00:54:50,080 --> 00:54:55,440
We were using the money we were making from Astropad Studio to canal the growth and development of Luna.

793
00:54:55,760 --> 00:54:58,240
Yeah, that had kind of become your like stone wall.

794
00:54:59,120 --> 00:55:00,640
It was a solid business at that point.

795
00:55:01,440 --> 00:55:01,760
Exactly.

796
00:55:02,240 --> 00:55:35,040
Which became important when the great reckoning happened with the great reckoning of so Apple came out with a feature for the iPad and macOS called Sidecar, which as the press would say, totally Sherlocked Luna Display and replaced, obviously, I think there's a well, there's definitely a very strong argument for why it didn't actually totally replace it, but in the eyes of if you're just looking at them, Apple came out with a built in and free way to do a similar thing.

797
00:55:35,840 --> 00:55:35,920
Yeah.

798
00:55:36,480 --> 00:55:39,440
And you've all covered this quite a bit.

799
00:55:39,600 --> 00:55:44,320
You've written some blog posts which are amazing and I'll link in the show notes.

800
00:55:44,720 --> 00:55:48,480
But I think the thing that I'm most curious about is why.

801
00:55:48,960 --> 00:55:50,880
How was that like seeing that happen?

802
00:55:51,120 --> 00:55:53,760
Like, had you heard rumors up to it that this might happen?

803
00:55:55,360 --> 00:55:56,160
No, no.

804
00:55:57,680 --> 00:56:03,920
We we heard the rumors pretty much the same time as everyone else did, which was great.

805
00:56:04,240 --> 00:56:10,720
I had actually taken some vacation, my wife and I had gone to France and we were in Paris and this is the same time.

806
00:56:11,840 --> 00:56:13,600
Oh gosh, what was the thing that burned there?

807
00:56:13,760 --> 00:56:16,720
I just totally Oh, Notre Dame?

808
00:56:17,360 --> 00:56:18,160
Notre Dame, thank you.

809
00:56:18,320 --> 00:56:19,680
I don't know why you spoke about that.

810
00:56:19,840 --> 00:56:21,520
You laughed and I was like, did I say something dumb?

811
00:56:21,680 --> 00:56:22,160
I can't remember.

812
00:56:22,400 --> 00:56:22,800
No, no, no.

813
00:56:23,040 --> 00:56:23,360
Okay, good.

814
00:56:23,840 --> 00:56:24,720
Notre Dame was burning.

815
00:56:25,440 --> 00:56:35,280
And it's like, and then shortly after it, it was like, we've heard this rumor that Apple was doing sidecar.

816
00:56:35,520 --> 00:56:38,560
And it was like, oh man, it feels like everything's on fire right now.

817
00:56:39,440 --> 00:56:44,560
And that was a couple of months after you had gotten Luna across the finish line, right?

818
00:56:45,200 --> 00:56:51,120
Yeah, yeah, this was a couple of months and Luna had been doing really, really, really well, really well.

819
00:56:52,000 --> 00:56:57,680
Was doing way better than even Astropad had done and just selling really, really well.

820
00:56:58,000 --> 00:57:03,600
We got covered by some YouTubers that just got us some great stu coverage and really boosted sales for us.

821
00:57:04,080 --> 00:57:06,880
We had our Black Friday was insane for us.

822
00:57:07,040 --> 00:57:08,320
It was our best, yeah.

823
00:57:08,640 --> 00:57:11,760
Best sales day of all time, still to this day.

824
00:57:12,800 --> 00:57:14,560
And things have been going really well.

825
00:57:14,800 --> 00:57:21,840
We were working on expanding the team further to kind of keep up with this demand, and we were building out more of a formal hardware team.

826
00:57:22,800 --> 00:57:26,080
And then that's when we heard that Apple was doing sidecar.

827
00:57:27,040 --> 00:57:35,280
And we were particularly sore about this as well because behind the scenes, we had been in multiple discussions with Apple as well.

828
00:57:35,680 --> 00:57:39,040
They had had us fly out to the campus and present what we were doing to them.

829
00:57:39,200 --> 00:57:41,760
So they were fully aware of everything we were doing.

830
00:57:42,480 --> 00:57:50,000
And they had promised support and had promised to help us with what we were doing and really encouraged us.

831
00:57:50,480 --> 00:57:53,680
And we saw them as helping a third party developer.

832
00:57:54,000 --> 00:57:54,160
Right.

833
00:57:54,400 --> 00:57:56,000
And also getting support from the App Store.

834
00:57:56,160 --> 00:57:59,200
We just saw it as them helping to improve their ecosystem.

835
00:58:00,000 --> 00:58:06,400
We were a bit naive in that because it turned out these same people we were talking to were also the people that were later involved in Sidecar.

836
00:58:07,440 --> 00:58:11,280
So while we were talking to them, behind the scenes they're actually working on copying it.

837
00:58:11,520 --> 00:58:11,680
Right?

838
00:58:12,240 --> 00:58:14,240
So we were pretty, we were pretty sore about that.

839
00:58:16,240 --> 00:58:17,840
And it was quite devastating.

840
00:58:18,160 --> 00:58:19,200
It was quite devastating.

841
00:58:19,600 --> 00:58:23,760
It just felt like the floor completely fell away under us.

842
00:58:25,120 --> 00:58:27,680
And we didn't know what to, we didn't know what to expect.

843
00:58:28,080 --> 00:58:31,520
Luna Sales went down a ton, it went down almost ten X overnight.

844
00:58:32,080 --> 00:58:34,800
This was when it launched or when the rumors started coming out?

845
00:58:35,200 --> 00:58:38,720
Well, the rumors started to hit and started to hurt.

846
00:58:40,000 --> 00:58:49,440
But when they announced it at WWDC and it was like a major headline feature, that's pretty much the day after that is when Luna Sales went down about 10x.

847
00:58:50,800 --> 00:58:59,120
Astropad didn't, yet, didn't take a hit yet, but later it did when Sidecar came out.

848
00:59:00,240 --> 00:59:01,920
And we took a really big hit on that.

849
00:59:03,600 --> 00:59:14,320
And we were just devastated, we were just devastated, and finally us being on only one platform, Apple's platforms had finally caught up with us.

850
00:59:14,640 --> 00:59:14,720
Yeah.

851
00:59:16,000 --> 00:59:19,840
And in hindsight, there were warning signs that we should have been paying attention to.

852
00:59:20,880 --> 00:59:23,920
We should have been more paranoid about it.

853
00:59:25,760 --> 00:59:31,120
But we were so focused on the day to day and growing LUNA and just holding on to what we had.

854
00:59:31,280 --> 00:59:38,240
It felt like LUNA was this wild buck that we were just like holding on to and just trying to, trying to keep things, just trying to keep the day to day going.

855
00:59:38,640 --> 00:59:42,240
And we had this massive code base and we're like, how are we going to get this to other platforms?

856
00:59:42,800 --> 00:59:44,160
Like we knew we needed to.

857
00:59:44,320 --> 00:59:50,560
And even actually prior to that point, we there had been multiple, I guess I would call them skunk work projects to try to.

858
00:59:51,920 --> 00:59:59,840
Like we spent a pretty much a bit of time trying to get Objective C working on Windows with the GlueStep project with Microsoft had a project for a while too.

859
01:00:00,080 --> 01:00:03,520
I can't remember the name of it right now, but to use Objective C on Windows.

860
01:00:03,920 --> 01:00:04,000
Okay.

861
01:00:04,400 --> 01:00:05,360
We had played around with that.

862
01:00:05,520 --> 01:00:09,200
So there had been multiple projects, but none of them had really panned out.

863
01:00:09,760 --> 01:00:18,960
So we were just, I mean, for me, it felt like waking up from a nightmare literally like the day after that WWC, like I woke up and I was like, man, was that a really bad dream?

864
01:00:19,520 --> 01:00:20,720
Was that a really, really bad dream?

865
01:00:20,960 --> 01:00:22,160
And it was like, no, that wasn't.

866
01:00:22,240 --> 01:00:22,880
This is reality.

867
01:00:23,040 --> 01:00:24,000
It was like, oh crap.

868
01:00:24,320 --> 01:00:26,240
Because it wasn't just Giovanni and I anymore.

869
01:00:26,560 --> 01:00:29,280
There were all these employees, too, on the team that depended on us.

870
01:00:29,840 --> 01:00:31,680
And we had contractors as well on top of that.

871
01:00:31,840 --> 01:00:37,840
So there's all these people that have been relying on us and we're like, oh no, like this is so scary.

872
01:00:38,000 --> 01:00:38,800
What are we going to do?

873
01:00:40,240 --> 01:00:44,800
So we pretty much immediately started an effort to develop for Windows.

874
01:00:45,120 --> 01:00:48,480
Pretty much the week after that WWEC, it was like, all right, let's go.

875
01:00:49,360 --> 01:00:53,840
And Windows in particular first, presumably, because that's where the demand is.

876
01:00:54,240 --> 01:00:54,800
Windows in particular.

877
01:00:55,120 --> 01:00:55,200
Okay.

878
01:00:55,520 --> 01:00:56,000
Windows in particular.

879
01:00:56,240 --> 01:01:06,320
Because we're like,re just going to have to stop really all significant updates on the Mac.

880
01:01:06,560 --> 01:01:11,120
We got to put everyone on getting us into Windows and just change our focus overnight.

881
01:01:11,760 --> 01:01:17,440
And actually, I even I even started tooting around a Windows laptop at that point.

882
01:01:17,680 --> 01:01:19,120
I was like, no, no more Mac.

883
01:01:19,520 --> 01:01:20,800
Like, I was using Windows all the time.

884
01:01:20,960 --> 01:01:21,840
I was running around with that.

885
01:01:22,480 --> 01:01:26,800
Partially, I assumed to get used to the platform, but I I imagine there's a little bit of spite in there.

886
01:01:27,360 --> 01:01:28,400
Oh, there was total spite.

887
01:01:28,640 --> 01:01:29,600
Yeah, there was total spite.

888
01:01:29,760 --> 01:01:30,320
Yeah, it was both.

889
01:01:30,320 --> 01:01:34,560
It was it was like it had been a while since I had been out of Windows, so I needed to get used to that.

890
01:01:34,640 --> 01:01:37,120
But it was also like, yeah, sticking it to Apple in some way.

891
01:01:37,360 --> 01:01:43,600
And that's also when we started writing articles and kind of documenting what was happening to us and talking more openly about Sherlocking.

892
01:01:44,000 --> 01:01:46,960
Because as we later found out, this actually happens to a lot of companies.

893
01:01:47,600 --> 01:01:48,480
This is not a new thing.

894
01:01:48,800 --> 01:01:48,960
Yeah.

895
01:01:50,080 --> 01:01:53,840
This is a lot, a lot of companies, companies significantly bigger than us as well.

896
01:01:55,280 --> 01:02:12,000
And kind of how things had played out where Apple would talk with you and gather information and then kind of do their own thing was not really new or unique to us, but we were able to talk openly about it because most others had some kind of constraints that prevented them from talking about it.

897
01:02:12,320 --> 01:02:15,200
It was either they were still super dependent on Apple.

898
01:02:15,680 --> 01:02:21,760
I mean, we were still dependent on Apple, but they had hurt our business so bad at that point that we really felt like we didn't have much to lose.

899
01:02:23,120 --> 01:02:27,200
Or they're investors or there's a parent company that owns them or something.

900
01:02:28,000 --> 01:02:30,800
Or a separate business that's tied to Apple that you don't want to see.

901
01:02:31,040 --> 01:02:31,600
Separate business.

902
01:02:32,000 --> 01:02:32,560
Yeah, exactly.

903
01:02:32,800 --> 01:02:35,280
For some, you know, for lots of reasons, I totally understand.

904
01:02:35,600 --> 01:02:38,880
Many of these people I would talk to in private and they would tell me their stories.

905
01:02:40,400 --> 01:02:42,240
And it was like, wow, that sounds familiar.

906
01:02:42,960 --> 01:02:45,840
But we were willing and able to talk about it openly.

907
01:02:46,320 --> 01:02:56,240
And so we did that leading up all the way to the House Subcommittee on Antitrust contacting us and wanting to hear about our experience.

908
01:02:56,640 --> 01:02:59,840
And if you go and look at the House.

909
01:03:01,280 --> 01:03:08,240
Report on antitrust, there's actually a section in there in the report on Sherlocking, which is pretty wild.

910
01:03:08,400 --> 01:03:23,760
It's obviously, it matters, but there's an element of like Sherlocking feels like this term from our weird little, you know, corner of the universe, and to see it like expand into a very important, you know, federal antitrust case.

911
01:03:24,240 --> 01:03:26,080
Yeah, it was it was it was pretty amazing.

912
01:03:26,240 --> 01:03:32,960
I mean, the other reason we spoke out too is because even before we were speaking out there was already a lot of pressureure on big tech and Apple in particular.

913
01:03:33,600 --> 01:03:36,640
So kind of the we, we felt the way the winds were going.

914
01:03:36,960 --> 01:03:37,520
We're like, okay.

915
01:03:38,080 --> 01:03:38,720
There's some safety.

916
01:03:39,120 --> 01:03:42,480
We can talk out about this and yeah, there's going to be some safety in the press.

917
01:03:43,120 --> 01:03:45,840
And that feels so like Sherlocking was a well known term.

918
01:03:46,160 --> 01:03:46,240
Yeah.

919
01:03:46,640 --> 01:04:18,800
But at least speaking personally, I can't speak for the whole broader world, but I certainly didn't realize that this practice of, you know, bringing people on and basically learning from the people that you're going to copy was a common practice and that feels that's where things get really nefarious because Sherlocking was always like, I feel really bad for these people, but the platform sometimes needs to build these things and like, it would be worse if they never did this, but I think that's I don't know, I think that was probably a really big public good.

920
01:04:19,600 --> 01:04:23,920
It probably has helped people, you know, maybe not to your knowledge already at this point.

921
01:04:24,240 --> 01:04:26,240
Well, yeah, no, and I and I hope so.

922
01:04:27,360 --> 01:04:30,000
And it actually functions really similar to Amazon.

923
01:04:31,600 --> 01:04:37,120
What I've learned is it's the same thing where Apple also has access to a lot of the analytics about what's doing well.

924
01:04:38,240 --> 01:04:47,600
And I've found that they don't, this hasn't just happened to apps on the App Store, but also to hardware makers selling stuff in the physical Apple stores as well.

925
01:04:48,000 --> 01:04:48,480
Oh, yeah.

926
01:04:48,640 --> 01:04:51,680
Where they'll be like, oh, this is selling really well in our Apple stores.

927
01:04:51,840 --> 01:04:53,520
Can we integrate this more into our ecosystem?

928
01:04:53,840 --> 01:04:54,560
Can we, you know?

929
01:04:55,360 --> 01:05:09,840
So the difference too when SureLocking started is Apple was a much, much, much smaller company and didn't control one of the most important platforms in the world.

930
01:05:11,200 --> 01:05:29,280
And now phones become such central parts of our everyday lives for everything we do, everything involves our phones now, our health data, our exercise, running, you name it, it's stored on our phones, right?

931
01:05:31,040 --> 01:05:36,720
These are super important devices in our lives and Apple has massive control over them.

932
01:05:37,200 --> 01:05:46,320
So that changes the game, I see, as well, compared to when Sherlocking started and Apple was much, much smaller.

933
01:05:46,480 --> 01:05:52,400
You know, it kind of reminds me of Apple's kind of like this puppy that grew into a giant dog, but doesn't know it's a giant dog.

934
01:05:53,040 --> 01:05:54,160
Still wants to jump in your lap.

935
01:05:54,560 --> 01:05:55,920
Yeah, it still thinks it's a puppy.

936
01:05:56,000 --> 01:05:57,440
And actually, this is my dog, right?

937
01:05:57,520 --> 01:06:00,880
I mean, he's not giant, but he's a basshound, so he's heavy.

938
01:06:01,040 --> 01:06:02,080
and he's a snuggler.

939
01:06:02,240 --> 01:06:04,080
Yeah, totally, totally.

940
01:06:04,320 --> 01:06:10,960
That's going to forever, every time I think about Apple as now the big player, that's going to be the mental image I have.

941
01:06:11,280 --> 01:06:16,000
Well, and I think, you know, the people on the inside too, they'd be like, they see themselves in the same way too.

942
01:06:16,160 --> 01:06:18,080
They're like, oh, we're still the scrappy upstart.

943
01:06:18,240 --> 01:06:18,320
Yeah.

944
01:06:18,480 --> 01:06:20,480
And you're like, well, no, not anymore.

945
01:06:21,440 --> 01:06:22,160
You're huge now.

946
01:06:23,120 --> 01:06:35,920
So, uh, as you said then, this, this whole experience, uh, totally, you know, crystallized the direction that you need to take, which was we need to get on Windows and we need to get on Windows yesterday.

947
01:06:36,320 --> 01:06:43,360
So like you said you had had a couple of efforts to try and figure this out but not obviously fully formed.

948
01:06:44,800 --> 01:06:46,480
What was your approach then from the get-go?

949
01:06:46,640 --> 01:06:51,440
Was it, let's try a hundred different things or did you really pick one and just go all in on this idea?

950
01:06:52,000 --> 01:06:52,880
Yeah, we picked one.

951
01:06:54,160 --> 01:07:05,840
Engineer on our team, Jeremy came with, he had been dabbling with this language Rust and he was like, you know, I think this could be a really good optiontion for us to develop our Cortex Liquid cross platform.

952
01:07:07,360 --> 01:07:09,680
And we kind of ran with that.

953
01:07:10,560 --> 01:07:12,400
He was really excited about it.

954
01:07:12,560 --> 01:07:17,760
It seemed like a great opportunity and we had to go forward with essentially a full rewrite.

955
01:07:18,320 --> 01:07:20,320
We had really exhausted the other options.

956
01:07:20,800 --> 01:07:35,760
We had tried to use what we already had and we knew that in order to actually make this work, that we were going to have to redo it in something that was more cross platform than Objective C and kind of the Coco UI Kit frameworks.

957
01:07:36,160 --> 01:07:36,240
Man.

958
01:07:36,720 --> 01:07:39,760
So we went all in because we knew that that was it.

959
01:07:40,000 --> 01:07:44,640
That was the one remaining way we could make this go and we really had no other choice.

960
01:07:45,360 --> 01:07:57,040
And I saw it was like a week or two ago, right, was your first public beta release for the Windows version of is it Astropad or is that Luna?

961
01:07:57,440 --> 01:07:58,400
Yeah, it's Astropad.

962
01:07:58,960 --> 01:08:00,080
We're calling it Project Blue.

963
01:08:00,640 --> 01:08:04,400
It's our free public beta of AstraPad for Windows.

964
01:08:06,320 --> 01:08:08,720
It's been almost two years now we've been working on it.

965
01:08:09,920 --> 01:08:26,080
It's close to a ground up rewrite of the whole thing with the core engine being in Rust, but it's turned out really, really well and now we've got this really solid platform we can build on in the future so we can develop our products on our cross platform on both Windows and Mac.

966
01:08:26,800 --> 01:08:30,080
The other thing that's been good is since Sidecar came out.

967
01:08:30,880 --> 01:08:35,040
We've found these areas that we really excel in that sidecar doesn't.

968
01:08:36,000 --> 01:08:39,360
And so it we've really stabilized the business as well.

969
01:08:40,240 --> 01:08:51,440
So and in fact in in recent months it's actually been growing on the Mac side of things as as we add new capabilities and new features and that sidecar doesn't have.

970
01:08:51,920 --> 01:09:06,080
Yeah, that's I mean that's always the advice for you know app developers whenever you get Sherlocked is you know know that Apple has a particular thing that they're targeting, which is mass market, not very deep, it's wide, right?

971
01:09:06,960 --> 01:09:12,080
And I guess it's a similar thing probably for you on the more hardware end of things.

972
01:09:12,880 --> 01:09:27,280
You were Sidecar is not targeted at creative professionals, and so that's an area where you have an expertise that you can really go all in, and like there's a low risk of Apple really following too deep, I imagine.

973
01:09:27,840 --> 01:09:30,480
Yeah, yeah, and we still even want to go deeper there.

974
01:09:30,960 --> 01:09:34,640
And really spend the time to make it work really well with certain programs.

975
01:09:34,880 --> 01:09:39,440
Like an area we'd love to explore more in the future is 3D programs, making it work better with 3D programs.

976
01:09:39,840 --> 01:09:46,320
We've really traditionally been focused on 2D drawing programs, but there's a whole world of 3D stuff out there, especially on the Windows side now for us too.

977
01:09:46,960 --> 01:09:54,640
Yeah, and almost everybody it seems like uses Wake Home tablets for 3D work and 3DS Max or whatever.

978
01:09:55,440 --> 01:09:56,480
Yeah, yeah, definitely.

979
01:09:57,200 --> 01:09:59,120
And so that's been really good for us.

980
01:09:59,280 --> 01:10:01,520
Like a lot of our customers have have stayed with us.

981
01:10:01,840 --> 01:10:06,800
We've heard from many customers that have gone over to sidecar and they're like, Oh, I really miss such and such feature.

982
01:10:07,040 --> 01:10:07,360
I'm back.

983
01:10:07,600 --> 01:10:08,320
It's a good feeling.

984
01:10:08,480 --> 01:10:09,760
Or this thing doesn't work as well.

985
01:10:10,080 --> 01:10:11,520
Yeah, no, it's a great feeling.

986
01:10:12,320 --> 01:10:23,360
And so knowing that we can do that, that we can expand that, we can build in features that sidecar is not going to have, and also go cross platform with it too.

987
01:10:24,160 --> 01:10:30,800
So go deep and go multi platform and really have a good feature ahead of us for both Astropad and Astropad.

988
01:10:31,120 --> 01:10:31,440
and Luna.

989
01:10:32,160 --> 01:10:36,400
And not to, you know, dive into future products or whatever, but sure.

990
01:10:36,960 --> 01:10:47,920
Is it even like a feasible thing to attempt to target like Linux or other platforms or is that, does it get so fragmented at that point that it would be kind of an insane thing to invest in?

991
01:10:48,800 --> 01:10:56,720
I would say it's possible if you maybe targeted specific distros or specific setups on Linux.

992
01:10:57,440 --> 01:10:57,920
It could be.

993
01:10:59,920 --> 01:11:01,440
But we'll have to see.

994
01:11:01,760 --> 01:11:01,840
Yeah.

995
01:11:02,560 --> 01:11:06,960
But your engine, at least, it sounds like, would be transportable or portable.

996
01:11:07,200 --> 01:11:07,760
Yeah, totally.

997
01:11:07,920 --> 01:11:12,000
And we've even had some internal stuff that almost runs on Linux.

998
01:11:12,800 --> 01:11:14,480
Nothing that's like production worthy.

999
01:11:14,720 --> 01:11:15,120
Right, right.

1000
01:11:15,360 --> 01:11:20,960
But yeah, because of the core, because it works on Mac and it works on Windows, it is certainly feasible.

1001
01:11:21,120 --> 01:11:23,360
It could, could work on Linux.

1002
01:11:23,680 --> 01:11:30,720
We'll just have to see where the customers take us, what they're really interested in, what the big requests are in the future.

1003
01:11:31,440 --> 01:11:32,080
That's awesome.

1004
01:11:32,240 --> 01:11:38,080
Yeah, and it sounds like you're on a, I mean, you were on a stable ground before, it's just that the stable ground got jackhammered away.

1005
01:11:39,120 --> 01:11:45,280
But it sounds like you're on a stable ground, not on a platform provider that can yank the rug out from under you.

1006
01:11:46,240 --> 01:11:58,240
Yeah, yeah, and we've been able to like repair it to over the, you know, and get ourselves back on stable ground over the past almost two years since, well, it's just been about two years since the first rumor of SAGCAR came out.

1007
01:11:58,560 --> 01:12:00,080
And we weren't sure at the time if that was going to happen.

1008
01:12:00,720 --> 01:12:03,680
But thankfully, yeah, we have been able to have been able to do that.

1009
01:12:03,840 --> 01:12:07,760
And, you know, our Mac products are still doing well and are going to continue to stay around.

1010
01:12:08,320 --> 01:12:11,280
That is, I just feel like this is such a cool story.

1011
01:12:11,440 --> 01:12:13,280
Uh, and it has a happy ending, which helps.

1012
01:12:13,600 --> 01:12:17,760
Um, yeah, yeah, which is, which is nice now because for a long time we didn't really know.

1013
01:12:17,920 --> 01:12:18,000
Yeah.

1014
01:12:18,400 --> 01:12:20,400
We didn't really know what that ending was going to be.

1015
01:12:20,800 --> 01:12:35,120
You've had, I mean, three major, uh, like heads down just really deep diving into something that you weren't even 100% sure was possible with the original Astro.pad, Luna, and then moving everything over to Windows.

1016
01:12:35,280 --> 01:12:36,640
So yeah, it's very true.

1017
01:12:36,880 --> 01:12:37,520
That's very true.

1018
01:12:37,760 --> 01:12:42,960
In a short company history, I feel like you have major arcs already.

1019
01:12:43,360 --> 01:12:43,920
Yes, we do.

1020
01:12:44,160 --> 01:12:45,840
Major ups and downs, for sure.

1021
01:12:46,480 --> 01:12:51,600
But it's, honestly, it's really inspiring and I've really appreciated the blog posts.

1022
01:12:52,640 --> 01:13:00,800
Just they've been really inspiring and set a good tone for a good way of running a business or thinking about the future.

1023
01:13:01,360 --> 01:13:02,560
So I really appreciate that.

1024
01:13:02,800 --> 01:13:02,880
Yeah.

1025
01:13:03,120 --> 01:13:03,680
No, thanks.

1026
01:13:03,840 --> 01:13:04,080
Thanks.

1027
01:13:04,240 --> 01:13:04,880
I appreciate that.

1028
01:13:05,280 --> 01:13:06,880
Good to know people are out there reading them too.

1029
01:13:07,040 --> 01:13:07,120
Yeah.

1030
01:13:08,800 --> 01:13:18,640
So I guess to wrap things up, I'll ask you the question I ask everyone here, which is, what's a person or people out there that have inspired you that you recommend other people to check out?

1031
01:13:19,520 --> 01:13:19,600
Yeah.

1032
01:13:19,920 --> 01:13:20,000
Yeah.

1033
01:13:20,240 --> 01:13:20,800
This is a great question.

1034
01:13:21,040 --> 01:13:22,480
And you did give me some time to think about it.

1035
01:13:22,560 --> 01:13:22,640
Yeah.

1036
01:13:22,800 --> 01:13:24,160
I know some of your guests.

1037
01:13:24,720 --> 01:13:27,200
I have less time, but you gave me more time.

1038
01:13:27,440 --> 01:13:30,480
And I was thinking of so.

1039
01:13:30,560 --> 01:13:41,520
So a couple, a couple of people that I've really been inspired by to check out on Twitter as well is Michael Fogelman.

1040
01:13:42,560 --> 01:13:47,200
He's a developer and also I would consider him like an artist.

1041
01:13:47,440 --> 01:13:52,080
He does these amazing projects and I love seeing what he's working on.

1042
01:13:52,880 --> 01:14:12,080
He's done a wide range of projects from things like building a NES emulator to doing a clone of Minecraft to doing all this, you know, programmatic art as well, where it generates just lots of really cool visualizations.

1043
01:14:13,280 --> 01:14:15,920
And you can find him at Fogelbird on Twitter.

1044
01:14:16,320 --> 01:14:16,480
Okay.

1045
01:14:16,640 --> 01:14:16,800
Yeah.

1046
01:14:16,960 --> 01:14:17,120
Yeah.

1047
01:14:17,600 --> 01:14:18,000
I found him.

1048
01:14:18,480 --> 01:14:23,840
And he's got this amazing, he even has a lot of the products he does as well.

1049
01:14:24,080 --> 01:14:26,000
He's turned into art that you can actually buy on his site.

1050
01:14:26,160 --> 01:14:26,720
Oh, that's cool.

1051
01:14:27,120 --> 01:14:27,440
Really cool.

1052
01:14:27,680 --> 01:14:28,080
Different things.

1053
01:14:28,960 --> 01:14:30,640
He works in just a wide range of.

1054
01:14:31,600 --> 01:14:39,040
languages, kind of crosses the discipline also between programming and pure creativity and art.

1055
01:14:39,600 --> 01:14:42,000
And so I just love to see what he's developing.

1056
01:14:42,160 --> 01:14:43,600
The guy is incredibly productive.

1057
01:14:43,920 --> 01:14:44,960
I don't know how he does it.

1058
01:14:45,920 --> 01:14:48,640
So I'm always inspired to see what he's doing.

1059
01:14:49,360 --> 01:14:59,360
Another one I love to see is Noel Berry, who does, he's a game developer, one of the people that did Celeste, which is this platform.

1060
01:15:00,880 --> 01:15:17,360
And the guy has just mastered the art of building games and just to see him put some pixels together and start to code something up and just to see how quickly he can put together like a platform game that just looks fantastic is is always inspiring.

1061
01:15:17,840 --> 01:15:20,480
Yeah, Celeste is, I mean, it's like a work of art.

1062
01:15:21,440 --> 01:15:22,560
Yeah, it's it's amazing.

1063
01:15:22,960 --> 01:15:26,320
Another one too in the related space is Eric Barone.

1064
01:15:26,720 --> 01:15:27,920
I think that's how you say Celeste.

1065
01:15:29,040 --> 01:15:29,360
I know this name.

1066
01:15:29,520 --> 01:15:30,480
I feel like I should know who this is.

1067
01:15:30,640 --> 01:15:30,720
is.

1068
01:15:30,960 --> 01:15:33,280
He's the sole creator of Stardew Valley.

1069
01:15:33,600 --> 01:15:34,400
Oh, yes, yes.

1070
